PART 1 — PRODUCT VISION AND STRATEGIC DIRECTION

1.1 Understand the problem statement

The selected HackFront India fintech problem statement is:

PS T4.1 — The Auto Debits Nobody Can Find

The underlying consumer problem is that people often see money leaving their accounts without knowing:

Which actual merchant or service initiated the payment.

Why the financial statement description is unfamiliar.

Whether the payment is genuinely recurring.

Whether the merchant is a processor or the underlying service.

What the original service promised to charge.

Whether the payment matches the service price.

What payment authorisation allowed the transaction.

Whether the payment mandate is active, expired or revoked.

How to cancel the service.

Whether the payment mandate must also be revoked separately.

Whether another payment occurred after the reported cancellation.

DebitLens must address this complete consumer journey.

1.2 Product identity

Product name:

DebitLens

Preferred tagline:

Find it. Understand it. Reconcile it. Verify it.

Core product positioning:

DebitLens connects what you paid, what you authorised and what the service promised.

DebitLens should evolve beyond ordinary subscription tracking.

It should become an evidence-backed financial commitment investigation and resolution platform.

1.3 Product differentiation

Do not attempt to differentiate DebitLens merely through:

A recurring-payment list.

A dashboard.

Reminder notifications.

A chatbot.

Subscription categorisation.

More graphs.

More numerical cards.

Those capabilities are available in various existing finance and subscription-management products.

The differentiated product journey must be:

Discover a potentially recurring debit.

Identify the underlying merchant using evidence.

Separate payment intermediaries from actual service providers.

Reconcile actual debits against known service terms and payment authorisation.

Explain discrepancies.

Guide service cancellation.

Guide payment mandate revocation separately.

Monitor subsequent debits.

Preserve a clear evidence history.

Generate a useful financial evidence summary that the consumer can retain.

The product should answer:

"I don't recognise this debit. Can DebitLens help me find out what it is, what I authorised, whether I was charged correctly and what I should do next?"

This must be the central user experience.

